# Frontend architecture

## Suggested layout

```text
frontend/src/
├── assets/
├── components/          # shared UI (buttons, inputs, layout)
├── composables/         # reusable Composition API logic
├── features/            # optional feature folders (users, roles, auth)
│   └── <feature>/
│       ├── components/
│       ├── views/       # or keep views under src/views
│       └── *.ts
├── router/
├── services/            # Axios API modules (one concern per file)
├── stores/              # Pinia stores
├── types/               # shared TS types / API envelopes
├── views/               # route-level pages
├── App.vue
└── main.ts
```

Use `features/` when a domain grows (auth, users, roles). Keep shared primitives in `components/` and `composables/`.

## Boundaries

- **Views**: route pages; compose components and call stores/services.
- **Components**: presentational or small interactive UI; keep focused.
- **Services**: all HTTP calls. Components/views do not call Axios directly.
- **Stores**: shared client state (auth, session UI). Not a dumping ground for every API call.
- **Composables**: reusable stateful logic without becoming a second framework.
- **Types**: API DTOs and envelopes live in `types/`; import them — do not redefine inline.

## Anti-patterns

- Oversized SFCs that mix layout, API, and business rules.
- Copy-pasted fetch/error/loading logic across components.
- New patterns (repositories, CQRS-style clients, heavy DI) without a clear benefit.
